#!/usr/bin/env python3
r"""warn-zsh-word-split-selftest -- prove the zsh word-split warning fires on the shapes that
go wrong, and ONLY on those.

Sibling of enforce-census-selftest, same harness: the hook is run by its shebang
interpreter (what settings.json runs), from a temp directory, with stdin as the payload.

WHY THE HOOK EXISTS (#1110). The Bash tool runs zsh, where an unquoted `$c` is ONE word.
On 2026-10-07 a probe ran `for c in "agent list" ...; do out=$(herdr $c --help 2>&1)`,
herdr got "agent list" as one argument, and a wrong finding was committed (dotfiles
c466be5, corrected in 09d4e57 with `${=c}`). That exact command is arm w01.

    uv run python3 ~/.claude/tools/warn-zsh-word-split-selftest
    uv run python3 ~/.claude/tools/warn-zsh-word-split-selftest --hook /path/to/copy
    uv run python3 ~/.claude/tools/warn-zsh-word-split-selftest --mutants

THE HARNESS PROVES ITSELF FIRST. c01 (the incident) must warn and c02 (`echo hi`) must be
quiet before any arm counts, so a hook that crashed or printed nothing cannot pass every
quiet arm for the wrong reason.

--mutants proves the arms can FAIL. It writes broken copies of the hook (each a plausible
bug: quotes read as bare, `${=v}` not seen, heredocs read, the jq shout dropped, ...),
runs every arm against each, and fails unless EVERY arm goes red on at least one mutant
and every mutant is caught by at least one arm. A mutant whose anchor text is missing from
the hook is a failure too, so the list cannot rot quietly when the hook changes.

Measured 2026-10-07, branch zsh-word-split-hook:

    the hook, /bin/bash 3.2 (its shebang)          69 checks, 0 failures
    the hook, bash 5.3 (copy with that shebang)    69 checks, 0 failures
    --mutants                                       54 mutants, each caught; all 69 arms red
                                                    on at least one

The first --mutants run found five arms that could not fail (three sat behind the hook's
cheap exit, one needed a "warns on everything" mutant, one a bad shebang) and was red
until they were fixed: the runner earning its keep. A replay of 1,996 real Bash commands
from 60 recent transcripts found BSD awk dying on a box-drawing character in a UTF-8
locale; the lexer now runs under LC_ALL=C, and u01/u02 with mutant M54 hold that.
"""

